note SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
note sha256.om: FIPS 180-4 SHA-256 as one function-mode unit, bound through the omega-bridge native
note call ABI (docs/NATIVE-ABI.md) and exported as omega_omsha. It replaces the algorithm of
note src/sha256.c: the C file keeps only the sha256_* entry points that cut caller data into
note pieces of at most 4096 bytes.
note
note The whole hash state lives in the caller's sha256_ctx (104 bytes, src/sha256.h):
note   bytes 0..31   state[8], uint32 little endian
note   bytes 32..39  count, uint64 little endian (bytes absorbed so far)
note   bytes 40..103 buffer, the pending partial block
note omsha c b n op o:
note   op 0  init: count 0, FIPS initial hash value
note   op 1  absorb b[0..n), n <= 4096, with src/sha256.c's buffering rules
note   op 2  final: pad, absorb the bit length, write the 32-byte digest into o
note   op 3  one shot: the digest of b[0..n), n < 4096, written into o; c is not used
note w is the caller's scratch for the message schedule (64 words, contents ignored and overwritten):
note Omega writes every word before reading it, so the caller need not clear it.
note n > 4096 traps 3; any other op traps 9.

note little-endian word of nb bytes at byte offset p of the context
fn getle c edit uchar 104 p ulong nb ulong to ulong
v = ulong 0
i = nb
while gt i 0 bound 8
i = sub i 1
v = or shl v 8 ulong get c add p i
end
ret v
end

fn putle c edit uchar 104 p ulong nb ulong v ulong to void
x = v
for i ulong nb
put c add p i uchar and x 255
x = shr x 8
end
end

note the hash words of the context into h, and back
fn loadh c edit uchar 104 h edit uint 8 to void
for i ulong 8
put h i uint getle c mul i 4 4
end
end

fn storeh c edit uchar 104 h edit uint 8 to void
for i ulong 8
putle c mul i 4 4 ulong get h i
end
end

note big-endian words of the block at byte o of b into w[0..16), then the schedule
fn schedb b view uchar 4096 o ulong w edit ulong 64 to void
for i ulong 16
q = add o mul i 4
v = ulong get b q
v = or shl v 8 ulong get b add q 1
v = or shl v 8 ulong get b add q 2
v = or shl v 8 ulong get b add q 3
put w i v
end
expand w
end

note big-endian words of the block at byte 40 of b into w[0..16), then the schedule
fn schedc b edit uchar 104 w edit ulong 64 to void
for i ulong 16
q = add 40 mul i 4
v = ulong get b q
v = or shl v 8 ulong get b add q 1
v = or shl v 8 ulong get b add q 2
v = or shl v 8 ulong get b add q 3
put w i v
end
expand w
end

note the 64 rounds, unrolled with the working words in locals (names rotate instead of moving).
note Same lazy low-32-bit cells and rotate trick as expand; K is an immediate; the eight words are
note reduced to 32 bits only when they are added back into h.
fn rounds h edit uint 8 w view ulong 64 to void
a = ulong get h 0
b = ulong get h 1
c = ulong get h 2
d = ulong get h 3
e = ulong get h 4
f = ulong get h 5
g = ulong get h 6
x = ulong get h 7
t = ulong 0
u = ulong 0
t = add x add get w 0 1116352408
u = shl e 32
u = or u shr u 32
u = xor xor shr u 6 shr u 11 shr u 25
t = add t u
t = add t xor g and e xor f g
u = shl a 32
u = or u shr u 32
u = xor xor shr u 2 shr u 13 shr u 22
u = add u xor b and xor a b xor b c
d = add d t
x = add t u
t = add g add get w 1 1899447441
